// Title: Potential Credential Dumping Via LSASS SilentProcessExit Technique
// Sigma ID: 55e29995-75e7-451a-bef0-6225e2f13597
// Status: test | Level: critical
// Author: Florian Roth (Nextron Systems)
// Date: 2021-02-26 | Modified: 2022-12-19
// ATT&CK: credential-access, T1003.001
// Description: Detects changes to the Registry in which a monitor program gets registered to dump the memory of
//   the lsass.exe process
// False positives:
//   - Unlikely
// References:
//   - https://www.deepinstinct.com/2021/02/16/lsass-memory-dumps-are-stealthier-than-ever-before-part-2/
//   - https://oddvar.moe/2018/04/10/persistence-using-globalflags-in-image-file-execution-options-hidden-from-autoruns-exe/
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/adf5a626c4a776f3f023056f0f398b9523a2f09e/rules/windows/registry/registry_event/registry_event_silentprocessexit_lsass.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language 12.4.1, live Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceRegistryEvents
| where RegistryKey contains "Microsoft\\Windows NT\\CurrentVersion\\SilentProcessExit\\lsass.exe"
